# ============================================================================
# WebTop - line ending normalization
#
# One rule: text is always LF, both in the repository AND on disk, on every
# platform (Windows/NetBeans, macOS, Solaris 11). Identical bytes everywhere
# means there is no conversion left to perform, so no two tools can compute it
# differently. That is why this uses eol=lf and not eol=native: with "native"
# the working copy on Windows goes back to CRLF and the conversion -- along
# with the disagreement it causes between the git CLI and JGit/NetBeans --
# comes right back.
#
# Exceptions are declared below, and only below:
#   eol=crlf  files that Windows executes (.bat/.cmd/.ps1)
#   -text     formats where CRLF is part of the format (iCalendar, MIME,
#             vCard, patches): their bytes must never be rewritten
#   binary    declared binaries, so we never depend on git's heuristic
#   -diff     text that is pointless to diff (minified files, source maps)
#
# Keep this file IDENTICAL across all WebTop repositories.
# ============================================================================

# ---- Default --------------------------------------------------------------
# text=auto: git tells text from binary, and text is normalized to LF.
# "text" is deliberately NOT forced per extension: should a file hold binary
# bytes (we have one, an .svg with lone CRs), text=auto leaves it alone.
*                       text=auto eol=lf

# ---- More readable diffs (per-language hunk headers) ----------------------
*.java                  diff=java
*.html                  diff=html
*.htm                   diff=html
*.css                   diff=css
*.scss                  diff=css
*.php                   diff=php
*.md                    diff=markdown

# ---- Text that is pointless to diff ---------------------------------------
*.min.js                -diff
*.min.css               -diff
*.map                   -diff

# ---- Must stay CRLF: Windows executes these -------------------------------
*.bat                   text eol=crlf
*.cmd                   text eol=crlf
*.ps1                   text eol=crlf

# ---- CRLF is part of the format: never touch the bytes --------------------
# iCalendar/vCard (RFC 5545 / 6350) and MIME (RFC 5322) mandate CRLF.
# This section is the reason a generic .gitattributes will not do for us.
*.ics                   -text
*.ical                  -text
*.ifb                   -text
*.vcf                   -text
*.vcard                 -text
*.eml                   -text
*.mbox                  -text
*.mime                  -text
# A patch carries the line endings of its context: rewriting them voids it.
*.patch                 -text
*.diff                  -text

# ---- Declared binaries ----------------------------------------------------
